
/*! \page about_programs_page Programs

\ref about_history_page  (Prev)   &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref about_features_page (Next)
<BR>

The PSCF package currently contains source code for two programs 
named pscf_rp and pscf_r1d. The purpose and capabilties of each
are summarized below;

<b>pscf_rp</b>:

The pscf_rp program can perform either SCFT or PS-FTS calculations 
for systems with periodic boundary conditions in 1, 2 or 3 dimensions.
This program provides capabilities for SCFT analogous to those of the 
older Fortran PSCF program, and also provides a complete set of tools
for PS-FTS simulations.  The suffix "rp" stands for "real periodic".

The pscf_rp program can be compiled to create either an executable that
uses only CPU hardware or a GPU-enabled executable that uses an NVIDIA
GPU, or both. The executable file for the CPU-only version is named
pscf_rpc, while the executable file for the GPU-enabled version is 
named pscf_rpg. Because these two executables provide identical 
capabilities, with identical user interfaces, they are described in
this manual as different versions of a single program that we refer
to by the generic program name pscf_rp. 

<b>pscf_r1d</b>:

The pscf_r1d program is a comparatively simple CPU program for 
one-dimensional (1D) SCFT calculations. This program can be used to 
treat 1D problems with planar, cylindrical or spherical symmetry, 
using a formulation of the modified diffusion equation (MDE) in 
Cartesian, cylindrical or spherical coordinate systems, and simple 
1D finite-difference spatial discretization.  It is useful for 
SCFT problems involving flat or curved interfaces, and for 
modelling spherical or cylindrical micelles. The pscf_r1d program 
uses standard CPU hardware, and cannot perform stochastic field 
theoretic simulations.

The pscf_rp program allows the user to choose between a continuous 
Gaussian thread model or a discrete bead-spring model of polymer 
conformations, while pscf_r1d can only treat the continuous thread 
model. 

<BR>
